{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for the better-corroborated theory is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive prediction of future success, but as a methodological rule for maximizing 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) and minimizing known error, accepting that this offers no guarantee against future falsification but represents the most rational non-probabilistic stance available.","counterconditions":["If preferring Theory A over B based on past tests logically entails assuming the future will resemble the past, then the distinction collapses into induction.","If Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude is logically incoherent (as argued by Tichý and Miller), then it cannot serve as the basis for rational preference.","If acting on a theory inherently implies an expectation of its future reliability, then any claim of avoiding induction is merely semantic."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument distinguishes between 'justification' (which requires induction) and 'critical preference' (which relies on the logical asymmetry of falsification). By treating corroboration as a report of past performance rather than a probability score, the scientist chooses the theory that has withstood the severest tests because it contains more empirical content and has survived attempts to refute it. This choice is a bet on truth-likeness, not a calculation of future frequency. The cost of this position is the acceptance of 'rational ignorance': we act on the best theory knowing it may be false tomorrow, without the comfort of assigning it a high probability of being true.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75}],"simulation_proposals":[]}